WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.900
You know the feeling you get
when you learn something new

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:06.300
about a health problem
you’ve been trying to reverse?

00:00:06.300 --> 00:00:09.780
Maybe high blood pressure,
diabetes, or heart disease.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:13.440
Well, there’s nothing I like better
than bringing you the information

00:00:13.440 --> 00:00:16.200
that will help you do just that.

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:18.780
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:18.780 --> 00:00:21.600
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:25.500
There are a variety of unique
health issues that women face

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:26.880
that affect their quality of life.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.400
And today we start
with one way to treat

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:33.724
one of the most debilitating diseases
that women face: endometriosis.

00:00:33.724 --> 00:00:37.380
Endometriosis is a
major cause of disability

00:00:37.380 --> 00:00:41.580
and compromised quality of life
among women — a chronic disease

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:45.420
which is under-diagnosed, under-
reported, under-researched.

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:49.740
For patients it can be a nightmare
of misinformation, myths, taboos,

00:00:49.740 --> 00:00:52.560
lack of diagnosis, and problematic
hit-and-miss treatments,

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:56.100
overlaid by a painful, chronic,
stubborn disease.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:00:59.340
Pain is what best characterizes
the disease,

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:03.120
pain, painful intercourse, heavy
irregular periods and infertility.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:06.180
About one in a dozen
young women suffer,

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:10.140
and it accounts for about half the
cases of pelvic pain and infertility.

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:14.100
It's caused by what's called
retrograde menstruation.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:18.300
Instead of the blood going down,
it goes up into the abdominal cavity,

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:22.500
where bleeding tissue of the uterine
lining can implant onto other organs.

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:26.460
You can have the lesions
surgically removed,

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:30.660
but the recurrence rate within
five years is as high as 50%.

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:35.700
Now, endometriosis is an
estrogen-dependent disease,

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:39.000
so might the anti-estrogenic
effects of the phytoestrogens

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.540
in flaxseeds and soy foods help,
as they appear to in breast cancer?

00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:47.340
I couldn't find studies on flax, but
soy food consumption may indeed

00:01:47.340 --> 00:01:49.380
reduce the risk
of endometriosis,

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:52.620
but I couldn't find any studies
on treating the disease with soy.

00:01:52.620 --> 00:01:54.900
There's another food,
though, associated

00:01:54.900 --> 00:01:57.840
with decreased breast
cancer risk: seaweed.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:01.500
Seaweeds have special types
of fiber and phytonutrients

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.560
not found among land plants,
so it's not like choosing

00:02:04.560 --> 00:02:07.140
to get your beta-carotene from
a carrot versus a sweet potato.

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:09.840
If you want these unique
seaweed components,

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.300
some of which may have
anti-cancer properties,

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:16.260
we need to find a way to incorporate
sea vegetables into our diets —

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:19.860
anti-cancer properties such
as anti-estrogen effects.

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:22.800
Japanese women have among
the lowest rates of breast,

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:24.780
endometrial, and
ovarian cancers.

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:28.260
They have longer menstrual cycles
and lower estrogen levels

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:30.180
circulating in their blood
and that may help account

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:33.420
for their low risk of
estrogen-dependent cancers.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:38.400
We assumed this was their soy intake,
but the seaweed might be helping as well.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:42.900
You can drip seaweed broth on
human ovary cells that make estrogen

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.440
and see estrogen levels
drop, because it's either

00:02:46.440 --> 00:02:50.160
inhibiting production or facilitating
breakdown of estrogen,

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.980
and may even block
estrogen receptors,

00:02:52.980 --> 00:02:55.740
lowering the activity of the
estrogen you do produce.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:03:01.560
This is in a Petri dish, though,
but it happens in women as well.

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:05.640
They estimate that an effective
estrogen-lowering dose of seaweed

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:08.460
for an average American woman
might be around 5 grams a day,

00:03:08.460 --> 00:03:13.800
but no one has apparently tried
testing it on cancer patients yet,

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:16.800
but it has been tried
on endometriosis.

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:21.600
Three women with abnormal cycles—
two of which with endometriosis—

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:27.000
volunteered to add a tiny amount
of dried powdered bladderwrack,

00:03:27.000 --> 00:03:28.980
a common seaweed,
to their daily diet.

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:34.440
It effectively lengthened their cycles
and reduced the duration of their periods.

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:39.720
And not just by a little.
Check out subject number 1.

00:03:39.720 --> 00:03:45.180
A 30-year history of irregular periods,
averaging every 16 days,

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:49.200
but adding just a quarter teaspoon
of this seaweed powder a day

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:51.900
added 10 days onto
her cycle, up to 26 days,

00:03:52.560 --> 00:03:55.560
and a half teaspoon a day
brought her up to like 31,

00:03:55.560 --> 00:03:57.720
nearly doubling the
length of her cycle.

00:03:57.720 --> 00:04:01.860
And they all experienced marked
reductions in blood flow

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:03.780
and a decreased duration
of menstruation.

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:08.520
Poor subject number 1 was
having periods every 16 days

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:11.520
that lasted 9 days long.
Can you imagine?

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:17.580
After 30 years of this kind of craziness,
just a half teaspoon of seaweed a day

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:21.240
and she was having periods just once
a month and only lasting about 4 days.

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:26.280
And most importantly, in the two
women suffering from endometriosis,

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:29.580
they reported substantial
alleviation of their pain.

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:31.080
How is that possible?
Look at their drop in estrogen levels.

00:04:31.080 --> 00:04:35.280
A 75% drop after just a quarter
teaspoon of seaweed powder a day,

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:37.740
85% after a half teaspoon.

00:04:38.280 --> 00:04:41.580
Now obviously with just a couple
women, no control group,

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:43.620
we have to do bigger
better studies.

00:04:43.620 --> 00:04:47.580
But look when this study was
published: more than a decade ago

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:50.340
and not a single such study
has been published since.

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:53.640
Does the research world
just not care about women?

00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:57.360
Millions of women are suffering
with these conditions.

00:04:57.900 --> 00:04:59.160
Who's going to fund it, though?

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:02.880
That much seaweed costs
less than 5 pennies,

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:06.360
so a larger study
may never be done.

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:12.401
But with no downsides, I would suggest
endometriosis sufferers give it a try.

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:13.740
In our next story,

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:16.260
we look at how oxidized
cholesterol found in eggs,

00:05:16.260 --> 00:05:18.420
processed meat,
and parmesan cheese

00:05:18.863 --> 00:05:23.158
can have cancer-fueling estrogenic
effects on human breast cancer.

00:05:23.527 --> 00:05:29.040
In 1908, the presence of
cholesterol crystals was noted

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:34.140
in the proliferating areas of cancers,
suggesting that perhaps cholesterol,

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:38.340
in some way, was associated with
the regulation of cancer proliferation.

00:05:38.340 --> 00:05:43.080
A century later we now recognize
the accumulation of cholesterol

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:45.660
as a general feature of cancer tissue,

00:05:45.660 --> 00:05:47.880
and recent evidence suggests
that cholesterol

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:51.480
may indeed play critical roles
in the progression of cancers,

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:55.080
including breast, prostate,
and colorectal cancers.

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:58.680
Perhaps that could explain why
egg consumption was associated

00:05:58.680 --> 00:06:00.480
with increased breast cancer risk.

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:03.540
And indeed, a systematic review
of the evidence suggests

00:06:03.540 --> 00:06:07.320
that dietary cholesterol intake
increases risk of breast cancer,

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:10.620
and the more cholesterol you eat,
the higher the risk appears to go.

00:06:10.620 --> 00:06:12.660
But why?

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:17.940
One thought is that the prolonged
ingestion of a cholesterol-enriched diet

00:06:17.940 --> 00:06:21.780
induces a chronic auto-
inflammatory response,

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:24.540
and we know that chronic
inflammation can lead

00:06:24.540 --> 00:06:27.780
to the initiation, promotion, and
progression of tumor development.

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:31.320
It's true that sprinkling some
cholesterol on white blood cells

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:34.620
in a test tube can trigger the release
of inflammatory compounds,

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:38.460
and LDL cholesterol can induce breast
cancer proliferation and invasion.

00:06:38.460 --> 00:06:42.900
But again that's in vitro, where you
can show that like breast cancer cells

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:46.500
can migrate nearly twice as
far within a day in a petri dish

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:48.420
in the presence of LDL cholesterol.

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:51.180
But what about in people?

00:06:51.900 --> 00:06:54.780
Well, the level of LDL cholesterol
in the blood of women

00:06:54.780 --> 00:06:57.720
diagnosed with breast
cancer does appear to be

00:06:57.720 --> 00:07:00.300
a predictive factor
of tumor progression.

00:07:00.300 --> 00:07:03.180
About two years after
surgery/chemo/radiation,

00:07:03.180 --> 00:07:07.020
not one of the women in the lowest
third of LDL cholesterol levels

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:08.400
had a cancer recurrence.

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:12.000
The same could not be said for
women with higher cholesterol.

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:16.620
We know cholesterol can cause
inflammation in our artery walls.

00:07:16.620 --> 00:07:20.820
Maybe it's also playing an effect on
breast cancer initiation and progression?

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:25.920
They speculate that the high cholesterol
levels may have a cancer-fueling effect.

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:28.140
And indeed, women
with breast cancer

00:07:28.140 --> 00:07:31.320
who happen to be taking
cholesterol-lowering statin drugs

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:35.460
appear to live about 40% longer
before the cancer comes back.

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:41.520
But the data isn't good enough to ensure
the drug benefits outweigh the risks,

00:07:41.520 --> 00:07:45.720
though lowering cholesterol
with diet one may be able to get

00:07:45.720 --> 00:07:47.340
the best of both worlds.

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:52.080
But what does that have to
do with dietary cholesterol?

00:07:53.160 --> 00:07:55.980
Sure, animal studies show that
if you feed mice cholesterol

00:07:55.980 --> 00:07:59.520
you can accelerate their cancers,
but extrapolation to humans

00:07:59.520 --> 00:08:02.940
is difficult as dietary cholesterol
has limited effects

00:08:02.940 --> 00:08:05.100
on blood cholesterol
levels in humans.

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:09.480
Thus, dietary cholesterol might
just be indicative of a lifestyle

00:08:09.480 --> 00:08:12.420
prone to health-related
problems, including cancer.

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:15.960
Maybe people are just more likely
to chase bacon and eggs down

00:08:15.960 --> 00:08:18.720
with a cigarette
compared to oatmeal?

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:22.740
It's hard to imagine how
dietary cholesterol alone

00:08:22.740 --> 00:08:25.440
could promote cancer development.

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:31.860
But that all changed recently with the
discovery that 27-Hydroxycholesterol,

00:08:31.860 --> 00:08:36.000
a metabolite of cholesterol,
can function as an estrogen

00:08:36.000 --> 00:08:40.200
and increase the proliferation
of most breast cancer cells.

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:45.780
Ah, so it's not the cholesterol itself,
but what it turns into in the body.

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:49.080
Scientists have long struggled
to understand why women

00:08:49.080 --> 00:08:52.980
with heart disease risk factors are
more likely to develop breast cancer.

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:55.740
Now, perhaps we know.

00:08:56.340 --> 00:09:00.840
The discovery that the most abundant
oxidized cholesterol metabolite

00:09:00.840 --> 00:09:03.360
in our bloodstream can
have estrogenic effects

00:09:03.360 --> 00:09:06.060
may explain the link between high
cholesterol and the development

00:09:06.060 --> 00:09:07.860
and progression of breast
cancer and prostate cancer.

00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:12.360
Yes, 27-Hydroxycholesterol
also stimulates the proliferation

00:09:12.360 --> 00:09:16.140
of prostate cancer cells, boosting
growth by about 50 percent.

00:09:16.920 --> 00:09:20.940
I've explored before the role
oxycholesterols may play

00:09:20.940 --> 00:09:24.360
in mediating pro-oxidative and
pro-inflammatory processes

00:09:24.360 --> 00:09:27.720
in degenerative diseases such as
Alzheimer's and heart disease,

00:09:27.720 --> 00:09:30.900
but now it looks like oxidized
cholesterol can play a role

00:09:30.900 --> 00:09:33.660
in all three stages of tumor
development as well:

00:09:33.660 --> 00:09:37.500
initiation, promotion, and then
the progression of the cancer.

00:09:37.500 --> 00:09:40.620
Not just promoting the growth
of breast cancer cells,

00:09:40.620 --> 00:09:43.020
but also inducing their
invasion and migration,

00:09:43.020 --> 00:09:46.200
potentially facilitating breast
cancer metastasis

00:09:46.200 --> 00:09:49.380
through suppressing
anti-cancer immunity,

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:51.660
and then inducing angiogenesis:

00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:53.820
helping breast tumors hook
up their blood supply.

00:09:54.840 --> 00:09:57.300
This is all supported by
several lines of evidence

00:09:57.300 --> 00:10:01.020
that point to a pathologic role
for this cholesterol metabolite.

00:10:01.020 --> 00:10:03.000
Yeah, you can feed
mice cholesterol,

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:06.240
their oxysterol levels go up
and their tumors accelerate.

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:09.480
It also appears to dramatically
hasten the spread, or metastasis,

00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:11.040
of breast tumors to other organs.

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:14.640
But turning to human
breast tissue samples,

00:10:14.640 --> 00:10:17.700
they found that more aggressive
tumors have higher levels

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:21.360
of the enzyme that converts
cholesterol into 27-HC.

00:10:21.360 --> 00:10:25.500
In breast cancer patients with estrogen
receptor-positive tumors, the 27

00:10:25.500 --> 00:10:29.820
Hydroxycholesterol content in their
breast tissue is increased overall,

00:10:29.820 --> 00:10:32.100
and especially within
the tumor itself,

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:34.740
so much so that
circulating oxysterol levels

00:10:34.740 --> 00:10:37.860
in the blood may one day
be used as a prognostic factor.

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:42.420
And breast cancer patients with low tumor
levels of the enzyme that breaks down

00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:47.160
27-HC did not live as long as
women who can detoxify it better.

00:10:47.940 --> 00:10:52.500
The bottom line is that some estrogen-
driven breast tumors may rely

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:55.800
on 27-HC to grow when
estrogen isn't available.

00:10:55.800 --> 00:10:59.820
And that may explain a second
breast cancer mystery.

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:03.960
Over 80% of breast cancers start
out responding to estrogen,

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:07.500
and so what we do is
use hormone blockers,

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:11.580
either aromatase inhibitors to stop the
formation of estrogen in the first place,

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:14.160
or tamoxifen to block its action.

00:11:14.760 --> 00:11:20.460
Despite the efficacy of these drugs, many
patients relapse with resistant tumors.

00:11:20.460 --> 00:11:23.520
And that's where oxidized
cholesterol can come in.

00:11:23.520 --> 00:11:28.020
27-HC can fuel breast cancer
growth without estrogen,

00:11:28.020 --> 00:11:31.980
which could explain why sometimes
these estrogen blockers don't work.

00:11:31.980 --> 00:11:35.340
And finally, 27-HC may explain
why breast cancer patients

00:11:35.340 --> 00:11:38.040
with higher vitamin D levels
appear to live longer.

00:11:38.040 --> 00:11:42.180
Vitamin D supplementation
decreases 27-HC levels in the blood.

00:11:42.180 --> 00:11:47.460
The best way, though, may be
to just lower overall cholesterol.

00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:50.880
Lower cholesterol, and you
lower oxidized cholesterol.

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:53.940
So discovering this role of cholesterol
is actually really good news,

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:57.060
since cholesterol is a highly
amenable risk factor,

00:11:57.060 --> 00:12:00.660
either by lifestyle, dietary,
or pharmacologic interventions.

00:12:00.660 --> 00:12:04.260
The implications of these findings,
according to the principal investigator,

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:07.920
is that lowering cholesterol with
dietary changes or drugs

00:12:07.920 --> 00:12:14.252
could reduce a women's breast
cancer risk or slow tumor growth.

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:18.060
Finally, when it comes
to uterine fibroids,

00:12:18.060 --> 00:12:22.080
is soy helpful,
harmful, or harmless?

00:12:22.080 --> 00:12:26.040
About 1 in 4 women will
eventually suffer from fibroids,

00:12:26.040 --> 00:12:28.380
most commonly manifesting
as excessively

00:12:28.380 --> 00:12:30.000
heavy periods and pain or pressure.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:32.280
Why might you feel pressure?

00:12:32.940 --> 00:12:36.600
Because you may be carrying
around 26 pounds

00:12:36.600 --> 00:12:39.240
of tumors in your uterus.

00:12:39.240 --> 00:12:43.260
Fibroids are the most common
reason women get hysterectomies—

00:12:43.260 --> 00:12:45.420
having their uterus removed completely,

00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:49.260
a major surgery associated
with disability and death,

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:52.860
but all surgery carries risk.

00:12:52.860 --> 00:12:55.200
The chances of dying
within a month of surgery

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:57.060
may only be about 1 in 1200,

00:12:58.140 --> 00:13:01.260
which makes it among
our safest surgeries,

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:04.080
safer than getting your
gallbladder removed, for example.

00:13:04.920 --> 00:13:07.500
But, of course, you lose the
ability to bear children,

00:13:07.500 --> 00:13:10.140
and costs billions of dollars a year.

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:12.240
Yet, despite the high prevalence,

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:15.540
significant pain and suffering,
and huge economic impact,

00:13:15.540 --> 00:13:18.780
relatively little is
understood about the cause

00:13:18.780 --> 00:13:22.560
and disease process that
lead to fibroid tumors.

00:13:23.220 --> 00:13:26.040
Avoiding atomic bomb
blasts whenever you can

00:13:26.040 --> 00:13:29.040
is probably a good idea in terms
of decreasing fibroids risk,

00:13:29.040 --> 00:13:32.520
but what about more easily
modifiable risk factors?

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:35.400
Well, alcohol consumption is associated

00:13:35.400 --> 00:13:37.740
with increased risk, particularly beer,

00:13:37.740 --> 00:13:41.580
and whenever you hear that,
whenever you hear beer specifically,

00:13:41.580 --> 00:13:44.460
you think of the hormonal
effects specific to beer,

00:13:44.460 --> 00:13:48.300
specifically the powerful
phytoestrogen found in hops.

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:52.020
Well, if that phytoestrogen
is increasing fibroids risk,

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:54.660
what about the phytoestrogens in soy?

00:13:54.660 --> 00:13:58.020
Well, this was looked at in
the Black Women’s Health Study.

00:13:58.020 --> 00:14:00.900
Fibroids are 2 to 3 times more prevalent

00:14:00.900 --> 00:14:02.100
among African-American women;

00:14:02.100 --> 00:14:04.440
so, they thought maybe dairy intake

00:14:04.440 --> 00:14:06.720
might be contributing to the disparity,

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:09.780
given their higher levels
of lactose intolerance.

00:14:09.780 --> 00:14:11.580
And indeed, dairy consumption

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:13.800
was associated with reduced risk.

00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:16.020
They figured it was the calcium content

00:14:16.020 --> 00:14:17.100
or maybe the vitamin D,

00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:19.860
but perhaps the women
were drinking soy milk

00:14:19.860 --> 00:14:22.620
instead and that was
increasing their risk?

00:14:23.160 --> 00:14:26.520
No, soy intake was found
to be unrelated,

00:14:26.520 --> 00:14:28.440
same finding in a group of
predominantly white women,

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:31.140
though they did note
a protective association

00:14:31.140 --> 00:14:33.840
with the amount of lignans
flowing through their bodies.

00:14:34.560 --> 00:14:36.660
Lignans are another
class of phytoestrogens

00:14:36.660 --> 00:14:38.880
found predominantly in flaxseeds,

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:40.860
but throughout the plant kingdom.

00:14:41.400 --> 00:14:42.900
Hard to make any generalizations

00:14:42.900 --> 00:14:45.120
about soy phytoestrogens, though,

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:48.240
as soy consumption was
rather low across the board.

00:14:48.240 --> 00:14:50.040
This was done in Washington state.

00:14:50.640 --> 00:14:51.780
If you go to Japan,

00:14:51.780 --> 00:14:53.460
where they have the highest per capita

00:14:53.460 --> 00:14:54.720
soy consumption in the world,

00:14:54.720 --> 00:14:57.240
you could get a bigger spread of intakes.

00:14:57.240 --> 00:14:59.700
The researchers had previously found

00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:02.160
that soy intake was inversely associated

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:03.360
with the risk of hysterectomy,

00:15:03.360 --> 00:15:05.040
meaning women who ate more soy

00:15:05.040 --> 00:15:06.600
had lower hysterectomy rates,

00:15:06.600 --> 00:15:08.460
suggesting a potentially protective effect

00:15:08.460 --> 00:15:10.320
of soy against uterine fibroids,

00:15:10.320 --> 00:15:12.540
since that’s the main reason

00:15:12.540 --> 00:15:14.400
women have their uterus removed.

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:17.760
This would be consistent
with in vitro studies

00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:20.100
that found that the
main soy phytoestrogen

00:15:20.100 --> 00:15:23.700
seemed to inhibit fibroid tissue
proliferation in a petri dish.

00:15:24.360 --> 00:15:25.980
But, when they specifically looked,

00:15:25.980 --> 00:15:29.040
there was no evidence of
a link to soy at all,

00:15:29.040 --> 00:15:30.360
protective or otherwise.

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:32.880
The same was found in
one study out of China.

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:35.460
Fruit and vegetable intake
was associated with

00:15:35.460 --> 00:15:37.500
significantly lower risk of fibroids,

00:15:37.500 --> 00:15:39.960
but soy food consumption was not.

00:15:40.860 --> 00:15:43.560
But a second study out of China,
published the same year,

00:15:43.560 --> 00:15:45.060
found a significant association

00:15:45.060 --> 00:15:47.640
between soymilk intake and fibroids.

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:51.780
That’s consistent with the
three alarming case reports

00:15:51.780 --> 00:15:53.760
of women with symptomatic fibroids

00:15:53.760 --> 00:15:56.940
reporting an “unusually”
high intake of soymilk,

00:15:56.940 --> 00:16:00.060
or regularly consuming
“excessive” amounts of soy,

00:16:00.060 --> 00:16:02.280
or “extremely high” intakes of soy

00:16:02.280 --> 00:16:03.360
every day for decades.

00:16:04.140 --> 00:16:06.240
It's hard to take these cases seriously

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:08.340
when nowhere do they actually say

00:16:08.340 --> 00:16:10.020
how much they were consuming.

00:16:10.020 --> 00:16:14.040
The only quantitative mention
was 40 grams of isoflavones,

00:16:14.040 --> 00:16:17.640
which roughly translates to 400 gallons

00:16:17.640 --> 00:16:19.200
of soymilk every day—

00:16:19.740 --> 00:16:23.580
that would be excessive,
but also impossible.

00:16:23.580 --> 00:16:27.480
The only way to know for sure
is to put it to the test.

00:16:27.480 --> 00:16:30.240
Not just a population
study or anecdotal reports

00:16:30.240 --> 00:16:32.940
but randomize women to two
years of soy phytoestrogens—

00:16:32.940 --> 00:16:35.940
the amount found in 3 to
5 cups of soymilk a day—

00:16:35.940 --> 00:16:39.360
and no significant effect

00:16:39.360 --> 00:16:44.403
on the frequency or growth
of fibroids was found.

